test.c was for my own testing purposes and can be ignored.
salloc.h is a header file I've had for a while that automatically checks 
allocation system calls for failure. 
The some MPI conventions and various helper functions (i.e. make_matrix, print_matrix) 
I have used in my solutions have been brought over from my time in CSC 364, which
had a sizeable unit on MPI, including an MPI distributed matrix multiplication 
lab (which is where I first wrote many of these hlepers). 

im2col.c represents my best attempt at a reasonable distributed algorithm for im2col

The basic outline is as follows:
  1. Create the M on rank 0 
  2. Allocate space for the rows of the resultant matrix (hencefore the Col)
    2a. Make sure the rows include space allocated for rows to be sent and recieved 
  3. Calculate the scatter offsets based on the size of M, calculate gather offsets
     based on the size of Col
  4. MPI_Scatterv() based on the scatter offsets
  5. Halo exchange the boundary rows 
    5a. MPI_Isend() my beginning rows to the previous process
    5b. MPI_Irecv() the rows from the next process to the end of my current rows
  6. MPI_Waitall() until the sending/recving is complete
  7. Perform the im2col operation on the rows allocated to this process
  8. MPI_Gatherv() based on the gather offsets (now in the size of Col), collected in rank 0
  9. On rank 0, check the generated Col against the sequential single-thread algortihm 
     provided in the specifications 
  10. free(), MPI_Finalize(), teardown

This algorithm represents my best understanding of how to efficiently share data
using MPI in order to perform the im2col operations. 

However, my best understanding is not good enough. I could not figure out how 
stride effects which rows must be shared via halo exchange for strides that are 
not 1. 

naive_im2col.c attempts to remedy that with a simpler (though less dynamic and 
interesting) algorithm

The basic outline is as follows:
  1. Create M
  2. Allocate space for Col (without rows allocated for halo exchange) 
  3. Calculate the gather offsets based on the size of Col
  4. MPI_Bcast() all of M from rank 0 to all other processes 
  5. Perform im2col operation on the rows allocated to this process 
  6. MPI_Gatherv() based on gather offsets, collected in rank 0
  7. On rank 0, check the generated Col against a sequential version 
  8. free(), MPI_Finalize(), teardown

Obviously, if the matrix is too large to be stored in memory, a likely motivation
for distributing this algorithm in the first place, then broadcasting all of M
is not feasible. However, this version only has to worry about the rows of the 
Col matrix to distribute amongst processes, instead of the confusing mapping of
scattering based on M's rows and gathering based on Col's different sized and numbered
rows. This simplification makes stride a non-issue, thus naive_im2col can handle
strides >= 1 (where im2col cannot).

RUNNING

There is a Makefile present in this project. 
$make
will generate the build directory with both im2col and naive_im2col binaries.
They can ran with 
$mpirun -np <number of processes> <im2col | naive_im2col> <M_width> <M_height> <K_width> <K_height> <stride>

Although there is a test setup as well with
$make test
Which will compare both im2col and naive_im2col, using variables defined in 
the makefile to test different sizes, kernels, and strides.

